\begin{center}
{
\Large
Shell Topic 07: sed
}
\vspace{0.1in}
\end{center}

\begin{note}
The \lstinline{sed} (\emph{stream editor}) program reads a text stream line by line,
applies an editing script to each line,
and writes the result to standard output.
Because it reads stdin and writes stdout,
it composes with the redirection and pipe operators from Topic 00.

The most common sed command is \emph{substitution} \lstinline{s/REGEX/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS}.
By default, substitution replaces only the \emph{first} match on each line.
The \lstinline{g} (global) flag replaces \emph{every} match on each line.
Notice that \lstinline{g} does not mean ``every line'';
sed always visits every line, and \lstinline{g} only controls behavior within a line.

The \lstinline{/} in \lstinline{s/a/b/} is not special.
Sed uses whatever character immediately follows the \lstinline{s} as the delimiter.
Therefore \lstinline{s;a;b;}, \lstinline{s|a|b|}, and \lstinline{s,a,b,} are all equivalent to \lstinline{s/a/b/}.
This is extremely useful when the regex or replacement contains slashes,
as is the case when rewriting file paths.
The expression
\lstinline{s/\/usr\/local/\/opt/}
is often called \emph{leaning toothpick syndrome},
and the identical but far more readable expression is
\lstinline{s;/usr/local;/opt;}.
\end{note}
